From: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
Subject: [PATCH] tee: qseecom: copy the request back when it is an inout memref

An application may answer in the request buffer rather than in the
response one. The FocalTech fingerprint application writes its result
into the request's header at +8 and sets bit 31 of the command id there
to say it answered; the Goodix one likewise treats the buffer it was
handed as working memory. The vendor clients see this because their
request and response are two regions of a single shared mapping.

This driver stages the request in memory of its own and copies only the
response back, so such an answer was lost. Copy the request back too
when the caller passes it as an inout memref, which the parameter check
already accepts.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
---
diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index 65e2dc7887ad..db82bae611f1 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -975,6 +975,18 @@ static int qseecom_tee_invoke_func(struct tee_context *ctx,
 		if (!ret) {
 			memcpy(rsp, b + rsp_off, rsp_size);
 
+			/*
+			 * An application may answer in the request rather than
+			 * in the response: the Goodix and FocalTech ones both
+			 * treat the buffer they were handed as theirs to write
+			 * back into, and a client that only reads the response
+			 * never sees that answer. Copy the request back when
+			 * the caller asked for it by passing an inout memref.
+			 */
+			if ((param[0].attr & TEE_IOCTL_PARAM_ATTR_TYPE_MASK) ==
+			    TEE_IOCTL_PARAM_ATTR_TYPE_MEMREF_INOUT)
+				memcpy(req, b, req_size);
+
 			off = PAGE_ALIGN(rsp_off + rsp_size);
 			for (k = 2; k < arg->num_params; k += 2) {
 				struct tee_shm *pshm = param[k + 1].u.memref.shm;
